@using System.Globalization
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Explore
@* Batch F2, 6-ARCH ("reuse the bits that we have! if you can't reuse them,
   then the bits need refactored for composability"): this card's own
   passage-grouping algorithm (Batch D) now lives in Explore/PassageGrouping.cs
   -- Groups()/SpanRef() below delegate to it so the popover platform's own
   shared passage-list component (PassageList.razor) reuses the EXACT same
   "what counts as a passage" rule rather than a second, parallel
   implementation. This alias keeps every VerseRun call site in this file
   (the @foreach markup below included) unchanged. *@
@using VerseRun = BibleAtlas.Client.Explore.PassageGrouping.VerseRun
@inject AtlasClient Atlas
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@* A parchment hover card floating over the atlas plate (design-direction.md's
   "popovers and hover cards are parchment artifacts on both pages" --
   lamplit paper over the world). World.razor owns the hover/close-delay
   state machine and just feeds this component whichever place is currently
   showing plus the screen point to anchor at (map.js reports that point as
   e.containerPoint, which lines up with this card's positioning context
   because #world-map fills .world-page via `inset: 0` with no border/
   padding -- see app.css).

   Batch D (batch-d-brief.md) rebuilt this card from direct, binding user
   feedback: no more bookkeeping. The per-(book,chapter) count-row index
   (the old MergedVerseGroups/verse-group-{BOOK}-{chapter} rows, WORLD-2/
   WORLD-8) is GONE -- the card is place name + verse content + controls,
   nothing else. This SUPERSEDES design-direction.md's "Hover place card --
   REVISED" section (which still describes that count-row index and a flat
   single-shot "Show N more") wherever the two disagree; that section is now
   stale prose, not a contract -- CONTRACT.md is.

   Batch E (batch-e-brief.md, time-accurate places) adds two more sections
   BELOW Batch D's verse content and controls, never disturbing them: a
   quiet blurb line and an instrument-face established/destroyed line, each
   date its own explorable node (Explore/YearNode.cs) opened via
   OnExploreNode. Title now reads Place.DisplayName (the scene's own
   period-resolved name), not Place.Name -- map.js/World.razor/PlaceNode
   follow the same swap; see wire.rs's ScenePlace.display_name.

   Verse content is now passage-aware rather than a flat capped list.
   MergedVerses() (unchanged -- every event's verse_groups' verse ids,
   flattened in event order then each event's own already book/chapter/
   verse-ascending group order, deduped) is the list this file and
   design-direction.md have both long called "canonical order". Groups()
   splits THIS list, by list position, into maximal runs of numerically-
   consecutive same-book/chapter verses: a run of length >=2 is a passage
   (renders as ONE block -- flowing KJV text, small inline mono verse
   numbers, a single span ref like "GEN.12.1-4"); a run of length 1 is a
   lone verse (its own mono ref line + serif text, same shape as before
   this batch). Grouping by LIST POSITION rather than a fresh global sort
   is deliberate: two verses that are numerically back-to-back but arrived
   via non-adjacent events never merge into one passage -- "the scene's
   activating verse list... canonical order" (the brief's own phrase) is
   this codebase's existing name for MergedVerses()'s own order, not an
   instruction to re-sort it (world-hover-text.spec.ts's black-box
   mergedVerses() mirror flattens the same way and calls it the same
   thing).

   _shownCount replaces the old boolean _expanded: how many of
   MergedVerses(), from the start, are currently rendered. It always starts
   at InitialShownCount() -- up to 4 verses if the FIRST group is a
   passage, up to 2 if it's a lone verse (CONTRACT's 4/2-initial rule) --
   and grows by PassageStep (5) or LoneStep (2) verses per place-card-more
   click, whichever kind of group contains the next not-yet-shown verse.
   The step is a flat verse count, not capped at that group's own remaining
   length, so one click can finish a passage AND spill into the next
   group's first verse(s) in the same step -- safe because rendering
   (VisibleGroups) recomputes fresh from _shownCount every time: a group
   only 1 verse into its reveal renders as a lone verse now and upgrades to
   a passage block automatically once a later click reveals a 2nd verse of
   it, with no separate reveal-history state to keep in sync. place-card-
   collapse snaps _shownCount back to InitialShownCount() in one click.

   Because World.razor renders this with no @key, hovering from one marker
   straight to another (no gap where hoverPlace goes null) reuses THIS SAME
   component instance with a new Place parameter, rather than
   destroying/recreating it -- OnParametersSetAsync's _lastPlaceId guard
   below is what resets expand/verse-text state for the new place instead
   of silently showing the previous place's already-fetched text.

   HOTFIX batch (batch-hotfix-brief.md requirement 1, user report: "if there
   are locations at the top of the screen and you hover for your hover
   menu, the hover menu can be cut off by the top of the screen" -- the card
   ALWAYS rendered above its marker before this batch, per app.css's own
   pre-existing `calc(-100% - 18px)` transform, with nothing to catch a
   marker too close to the container's own top edge). X/Y stay the raw
   marker anchor point, unchanged; app.css's `.place-card` transform now
   reads CSS custom properties this file sets inline -- --card-dx/--card-dy/
   --card-dy-adjust -- computed by CardPlacement.Compute (client/
   CardPlacement.cs, a standalone pure function -- see its own header for
   why it isn't a method here) from a JS-measured render box
   (MapInterop.MeasureCardPlacement). Deliberately a SNAPSHOT decision, not
   a continuously-tracked one ("Flip decision at open time... recompute on
   re-open, not on pan" -- the brief's own words): OnAfterRenderAsync below
   only measures once per genuinely NEW place (keyed off the same Place.Id
   change OnParametersSetAsync's own _lastPlaceId guard already detects),
   and only once this place's own chapter-text/history fetches have BOTH
   settled (_contentReady) -- measuring any earlier would size against
   not-yet-loaded content (LoadHistory's blurb/dates sections specifically,
   entirely absent from the DOM until it resolves) and risk exactly the
   under-measurement this fix exists to prevent. Until that first
   measurement lands, CardDxCss/CardDyCss/CardDyAdjustCss below (Blazor-
   side, not a CSS var() fallback -- see app.css's own comment on
   .place-card for why) return the exact pre-batch values, so the card
   renders at its ORIGINAL default position (a plain center-above placement
   byte-identical to today) -- so the common,
   fast-cache case (LoadChaptersFor/LoadHistory both resolve well inside a
   single frame) never shows anything BUT the final, correct placement, and
   even a slow-network open only ever shows a BRIEF default-placed frame
   before self-correcting, never a permanently wrong one. The existing
   hover-persistence/grace machinery below (_pointerOverCard et al, owned by
   World.razor) is completely unaware of any of this -- OnPointerEnter/
   OnPointerLeave fire off this component's OWN root element regardless of
   which side of the marker it currently renders on, so the corridor works
   identically in both orientations with no changes needed here or there.

   Fix round 1 (review finding, Important): the initial cut of this fix
   only ever asked "does the card fit ABOVE the marker" -- a flipped card
   was never checked against the container's own BOTTOM edge, so a tall
   enough card near the vertical middle of a short viewport (e.g. 1280x720,
   Playwright's own default) could flip below and overflow past the
   bottom -- live-reproduced by review at 122px, a real marker ("Ai",
   exodus scene), the exact user-reported bug class on the opposite edge.
   CardPlacement.Compute now takes containerHeight too and, when NEITHER
   orientation fully fits, picks whichever shows more of the card and
   clamps it inside the container (--card-dy-adjust, a plain pixel nudge
   layered on top of --card-dy the same way --card-dx layers on top of the
   native -50% horizontal centering) -- see that class's own header for the
   full decision table. Still a snapshot, decided at the same single
   measurement point as the flip/horizontal-clamp decisions above -- no new
   timing/re-measurement concerns introduced. *@

<div class="place-card"
     data-testid="place-card"
     data-pinned="@(Pinned ? "true" : "false")"
     data-flip="@(_flipped ? "true" : "false")"
     @ref="_cardEl"
     style="left: @X.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)px; top: @Y.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)px; --card-dx: @(CardDxCss)px; --card-dy: @(CardDyCss); --card-dy-from: @(CardDyFromCss); --card-dy-adjust: @(CardDyAdjustCss)px;"
     @onpointerenter="OnPointerEnter"
     @onpointerleave="OnPointerLeave">
    <div class="place-card-scroll">
        <div class="place-card-head">
            <button type="button" class="place-card-title explorable" data-testid="place-card-title" @onclick="() => OnOpenPlace.InvokeAsync()">@Place.DisplayName</button>
            @* Batch G1 requirement 3: present only while pinned -- a hover-
               only card already closes itself on pointer-leave, so an
               explicit close control would be redundant/confusing there;
               CONTRACT's own "closes via Escape, an X, or clicking
               elsewhere on the map" is specifically about the pinned case. *@
            @if (Pinned)
            {
                <button type="button" class="place-card-close" data-testid="place-card-close"
                        aria-label="Close" @onclick="() => OnClosePin.InvokeAsync()">&times;</button>
            }
        </div>

        @{
            var verses = MergedVerses();
            var visible = VisibleGroups(Groups(verses), _shownCount);
        }
        @if (verses.Count == 0)
        {
            @* Batch E2 (the ever-present graph): Place.Events is empty ONLY
               for a quiet place -- World.razor's QuietAsScenePlace is the
               one path that ever constructs a ScenePlace with no events;
               SCENE-2 guarantees every REAL lit place has >=1, so this
               branch can never fire for one. Exact text, CONTRACT-documented
               (batch-e2-brief.md Requirement 3). *@
            <p class="place-card-quiet" data-testid="place-card-quiet">No recorded events in this window &mdash; drag the timeline.</p>
        }
        else
        {
            @* Batch G1 requirement 1b (user 2026-08-19: "verses/passages
               viewed in the map are not explorable... this is all one big
               dag that we can explore from anywhere"): every hover-verse-
               {VREF}/hover-passage-{SPAN} element becomes explorable under
               the SAME one rule (.explorable, app.css) -- hover/focus
               darkens, click (or Enter) opens the ExplorerPopover via
               OnExploreNode, the EXACT SAME callback/mechanism Batch E's
               date affordances already use (World.razor's OpenExploreNode:
               push the node, close this hover card the way a promoted
               popover always supersedes it). A passage block nests its own
               per-verse spans inside the block-level passage row -- clicking
               a SPECIFIC verse's span opens just that VerseNode and stops
               the click from also bubbling into the passage row's own
               handler (@onclick:stopPropagation); clicking anywhere else in
               the row (the ref label, or the block's own whitespace) opens
               the whole PassageNode instead. place-card-more/-collapse
               (below, outside this loop) stay non-explorable controls,
               unchanged. *@
            @foreach (var g in visible)
            {
                @if (g.IsPassage)
                {
                    var blockVerses = verses.GetRange(g.Start, g.Length);
                    var span = SpanRef(blockVerses[0], blockVerses[^1]);
                    <div class="place-card-passage explorable" data-testid="hover-passage-@span"
                         tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="@($"Explore passage {span}")"
                         @onclick="() => ExplorePassage(span, blockVerses)"
                         @onkeydown="@(e => OnPassageKeyDown(e, span, blockVerses))">
                        <span class="place-card-passage-ref">@span</span>
                        <span class="place-card-passage-text">
                            @foreach (var vref in blockVerses)
                            {
                                <span class="place-card-passage-verse explorable" data-testid="hover-verse-@vref"
                                      tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="@($"Explore {vref}")"
                                      @onclick="() => ExploreVerse(vref)" @onclick:stopPropagation="true"
                                      @onkeydown="@(e => OnVerseKeyDown(e, vref))" @onkeydown:stopPropagation="true"
                                      ><sup class="place-card-verse-num">@VerseNumOf(vref)</sup>@VerseText(vref) </span>
                            }
                        </span>
                    </div>
                }
                else
                {
                    var vref = verses[g.Start];
                    <div class="place-card-verse explorable" data-testid="hover-verse-@vref"
                         tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="@($"Explore {vref}")"
                         @onclick="() => ExploreVerse(vref)" @onkeydown="@(e => OnVerseKeyDown(e, vref))">
                        <span class="place-card-verse-ref">@vref</span>
                        <span class="place-card-verse-text">@VerseText(vref)</span>
                    </div>
                }
            }
        }
    </div>

    @if (_shownCount > _initialShownCount || _shownCount < verses.Count)
    {
        <div class="place-card-controls">
            @if (_shownCount > _initialShownCount)
            {
                <button type="button" class="place-card-collapse" data-testid="place-card-collapse"
                        aria-label="Show fewer verses" @onclick="Collapse">&#9652;</button>
            }
            @if (_shownCount < verses.Count)
            {
                <button type="button" class="place-card-more" data-testid="place-card-more"
                        aria-label="Show more verses" @onclick="ShowMore">&#9662;</button>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @* Batch E additions -- strictly BELOW Batch D's verse content and
       controls above (nothing renders after this), per batch-e-brief.md
       Requirement 4. Conditional presence throughout: no blurb section
       when the API returned none for this window; no dates section when
       neither established nor destroyed is curated for this place. *@
    @if (_history?.Blurb is { } blurb)
    {
        <p class="place-card-blurb" data-testid="place-card-blurb">@blurb</p>
    }

    @{
        var established = _history?.Established;
        var destroyed = _history?.Destroyed;
    }
    @if (established is not null || destroyed is not null)
    {
        <div class="place-card-dates" data-testid="place-card-dates">
            @if (established is { } est)
            {
                var estLabel = ExploreLabel("Established", est);
                <button type="button" class="place-card-date explorable" data-testid="place-card-date-established"
                        aria-label="@estLabel"
                        @onclick="() => ExploreEstablished(est)">
                    <span class="place-card-date-label">Established</span>
                    <span class="place-card-date-value">@DateText(est)</span>
                    <span class="place-card-date-explore" aria-hidden="true">Explore</span>
                </button>
            }
            @if (established is not null && destroyed is not null)
            {
                <span class="place-card-dates-sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
            }
            @if (destroyed is { } dest)
            {
                var destLabel = ExploreLabel("Destroyed", dest);
                <button type="button" class="place-card-date explorable" data-testid="place-card-date-destroyed"
                        aria-label="@destLabel"
                        @onclick="() => ExploreDestroyed(dest)">
                    <span class="place-card-date-label">Destroyed</span>
                    <span class="place-card-date-value">@DateText(dest)</span>
                    <span class="place-card-date-explore" aria-hidden="true">Explore</span>
                </button>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @* Batch G1 requirement 3 (narrative traversal) -- strictly LAST, below
       every Batch D/E section above, same "later batches append below,
       never disturb" layering. One row per narrative that has >=1 arrow
       touching THIS place in the CURRENT scene; entirely absent when
       Arrows/Narratives are null (scripture mode, or a scene not yet
       loaded) or this place has no leg of any narrative here.

       Fix round 1 (batch-g1-review.md M1): also gated on Pinned. NarrativeRows()
       is a pure function of Arrows/Narratives/Place alone -- it doesn't know or
       care whether this card is pinned -- and World.razor passes Arrows/
       Narratives into this component unconditionally (see its PlaceCard
       invocation), same as every other parameter. Without an explicit Pinned
       check here, a plain unpinned HOVER over a place with narrative legs
       rendered the exact same fully-functional traversal controls a deliberate
       click-to-pin is supposed to unlock -- verified live by the reviewer
       (hovering marker-rameses in the exodus window left data-pinned="false"
       but card-next-event-exodus present and clickable), and a direct
       contradiction of this file's own place-card-close gate a few lines above
       (~85) plus CONTRACT.md's already-correct "present ONLY when the pinned
       place has >=1 narrative leg" text. *@
    @{ var narrativeRows = NarrativeRows(); }
    @if (Pinned && narrativeRows.Count > 0)
    {
        <div class="place-card-narratives" data-testid="place-card-narratives">
            @foreach (var row in narrativeRows)
            {
                <div class="place-card-narrative-row">
                    <span class="place-card-narrative-swatch" style="background-color: @row.Color;" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <span class="place-card-narrative-name">@row.Name</span>
                    @if (row.PrevPlaceId is { } prevId)
                    {
                        <button type="button" class="place-card-nav-event" data-testid="card-prev-event-@row.NarrativeId"
                                aria-label="@($"Previous event in {row.Name}")"
                                @onclick="() => OnGoToPlace.InvokeAsync(prevId)">&lsaquo; previous event</button>
                    }
                    @if (row.NextPlaceId is { } nextId)
                    {
                        <button type="button" class="place-card-nav-event" data-testid="card-next-event-@row.NarrativeId"
                                aria-label="@($"Next event in {row.Name}")"
                                @onclick="() => OnGoToPlace.InvokeAsync(nextId)">next event &rsaquo;</button>
                    }
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ScenePlace Place { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public double X { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public double Y { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnPointerEnter { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnPointerLeave { get; set; }

    // Batch E: the scene's own active window (time mode) or null (scripture
    // mode / not yet applied) -- forwarded to AtlasClient.PlaceHistory so
    // the fetched blurb/display-name resolve against the SAME window the
    // map itself is currently showing. World.razor is the source of truth
    // (its own _from/_to fields), same "page owns state, card just renders
    // it" split every other PlaceCard parameter already follows.
    [Parameter] public int? WindowFrom { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public int? WindowTo { get; set; }

    // Batch E: fires with a freshly-constructed YearNode when a date
    // affordance (place-card-date-established/-destroyed) is activated --
    // World.razor wires this to push the node onto its own ExplorerPopover,
    // the same "card promotes into a real popover" pattern OnOpenPlace
    // already uses for the title/PlaceNode.
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<IExplorable> OnExploreNode { get; set; }

    // WORLD-8/Task 15: the title still promotes this hover card's content
    // into a real ExplorerPopover -- World.razor already has Place.Id/
    // Place.Name in hand from _hoverPlace, the same source this
    // component's own Place parameter comes from, so this stays
    // parameterless rather than passing a whole node instance across the
    // component boundary. Batch D removed this card's OTHER, older route
    // into a popover -- the verse-group index rows' click-to-open-a-verse
    // affordance -- along with the rows themselves; the brief's own content
    // list for this card is closed ("place name + verse content +
    // controls, nothing else"), so there is no replacement for it here.
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnOpenPlace { get; set; }

    // Batch G1 requirement 3 (click-to-pin/narrative traversal). Pinned:
    // World.razor's own source of truth (its _pinnedPlaceId field) for
    // whether THIS currently-shown card is pinned -- drives the close
    // button's conditional presence and the place-card data-pinned
    // attribute. Narratives: the current scene's own legend list (null in
    // scripture mode / before the first scene loads) -- read-only, used
    // solely for each narrative's own swatch Color now (see NarrativeRows()
    // below); never mutated here, World.razor owns the scene. OnGoToPlace
    // fires with the ADJACENT place's own id when a ‹ previous event / next
    // event › control is clicked; OnClosePin fires when the card's own "X"
    // is clicked.
    //
    // Fix round 1 (batch-n-review.md Critical-1): the Arrows parameter
    // (the current scene's own WINDOWED arrow list) that NarrativeRows()
    // used to derive prev/next adjacency from is GONE -- the review proved
    // that windowed derivation can disagree with the verse popover's own
    // unwindowed positions_for_events resolver (batch-n-brief.md) for an
    // entirely ordinary time-slider window (see LoadNarrativePositions'
    // own comment below for the full mechanism and the real curated-data
    // repro). World.razor's own <PlaceCard> call site no longer passes it.
    [Parameter] public bool Pinned { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public List<SceneNarrative>? Narratives { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> OnGoToPlace { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnClosePin { get; set; }

    // CONTRACT's 4/2-initial + 5/2-step rule (batch-d-brief.md Requirement 2).
    private const int PassageInitialMax = 4;
    private const int PassageStep = 5;
    private const int LoneInitialMax = 2;
    private const int LoneStep = 2;

    // Identifies which place the fetched/fetching _verseText belongs to, so
    // a hover that moves marker-to-marker (see the file-header comment)
    // resets rather than showing stale text/reveal-state under the new
    // place's name.
    private string? _lastPlaceId;
    private int _shownCount;
    private int _initialShownCount;
    private readonly Dictionary<string, string> _verseText = new();

    // Batch E: this place's window-scoped history payload (null while
    // loading, still-loaded, or simply absent for a place with no curated
    // history -- all three render the same "no blurb/dates section" way,
    // per CONTRACT's conditional-presence rule).
    private PlaceHistoryOut? _history;

    // Fix round 1 (batch-n-review.md Critical-1): every narrative position
    // (atlas_core::narrative::NarrativePosition, wire NarrativePositionDto)
    // touching any event CURRENTLY shown at this place -- populated by
    // LoadNarrativePositions below, consumed synchronously by
    // NarrativeRows(). Empty (not null) both before the first fetch settles
    // and for a place with no narrative-leg events, same "always a list,
    // conditional presence via emptiness" convention _verseText/_history
    // already follow.
    private List<NarrativePositionDto> _narrativePositions = new();

    // HOTFIX batch (requirement 1, flip-below-when-cut-off): the card's own
    // root element, measured post-render by OnAfterRenderAsync below via
    // MapInterop.MeasureCardPlacement -- see the file header comment for
    // the full mechanism and why this is a deliberate one-shot-per-open
    // snapshot, not a continuous tracker.
    private ElementReference _cardEl;

    // True once BOTH LoadChaptersFor and LoadHistory have settled for the
    // CURRENT place (OnParametersSetAsync's own Task.WhenAll below) -- the
    // earliest point at which this card's own rendered height reflects
    // every CONDITIONALLY-present section (blurb/dates specifically) it
    // will ever have for this open, so the earliest point a measurement can
    // be trusted. Reset to false alongside _lastPlaceId on every genuinely
    // new place.
    private bool _contentReady;

    // Which place _dxPx/_dyAdjustPx/_flipped's own numbers were measured
    // FOR -- distinct from _lastPlaceId (that one gates the verse-text/
    // history fetch reset; this one gates the separate, async-JS-interop-
    // driven measurement below, which can legitimately still be in flight
    // after _lastPlaceId has already moved on to a newer place -- see
    // OnAfterRenderAsync's own stale-result guard). Null (never measured
    // yet) renders at the default CSS placement -- see CardDxCss/CardDyCss
    // below.
    private string? _placedForId;
    private double _dxPx;
    private bool _flipped;

    // Fix round 1 (review finding, Important): the vertical counterpart of
    // _dxPx -- a plain pixel nudge CardPlacement.Compute returns whenever
    // the CHOSEN orientation (above or below, per _flipped) still doesn't
    // fully fit the container's own edge on that side (e.g. a flipped card
    // near the vertical middle of a short viewport, which the pre-fix-
    // round-1 code never checked at all -- see CardPlacement.cs's own
    // header for the live-reproduced 122px overflow this closes). Zero for
    // the overwhelming common case (the chosen side already fits cleanly).
    private double _dyAdjustPx;

    // app.css's `.place-card` transform (its resting state) AND its
    // `@keyframes place-card-in` (its entrance animation, both stops --
    // see that rule's own comment for why it needs the SAME custom
    // properties, not the flat hardcoded expression it used to) read
    // --card-dx/--card-dy/--card-dy-from/--card-dy-adjust unconditionally,
    // no CSS-side var() fallback -- these four always supply a concrete
    // value, "0" / the pre-batch default vertical calc() before the first
    // real measurement lands, so a not-yet-measured card renders EXACTLY
    // where every card always rendered before this batch.
    private bool IsFlippedNow => _placedForId == Place.Id && _flipped;
    private string CardDxCss => (_placedForId == Place.Id ? _dxPx : 0).ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
    private string CardDyCss => VerticalCss(CardPlacement.GapPx);

    // The entrance animation's OWN "from" stop -- Batch D's original
    // pop-in settled down 8px (10px -> 18px, above the marker); this
    // mirrors that exact nuance for a flipped card too (10px -> 18px,
    // BELOW the marker instead), keyed off the SAME flip decision as
    // CardDyCss, just a smaller gap.
    private string CardDyFromCss => VerticalCss(CardPlacement.GapPx - 8);

    // Fix round 1: layered on top of BOTH --card-dy and --card-dy-from
    // (see app.css's own `calc(var(--card-dy) + var(--card-dy-adjust))`) --
    // applying the SAME adjustment to both keeps the animation's own
    // "settle down 8px" motion intact while sliding the whole thing (start
    // and end alike) into the clamped, fully-on-screen final position, so
    // there's no visible overshoot/snap once the entrance animation ends.
    private string CardDyAdjustCss => (_placedForId == Place.Id ? _dyAdjustPx : 0).ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

    private string VerticalCss(double gapPx)
    {
        var gap = gapPx.ToString(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
        return IsFlippedNow ? $"{gap}px" : $"calc(-100% - {gap}px)";
    }

    // The flat, deduped verse-id list the card renders from -- unchanged
    // since before this batch: event order, then each event's own already
    // (book,chapter)-then-verse-number sorted VerseGroups (see atlas-core's
    // verse_groups_for), deduped (a verse can legitimately anchor more than
    // one event at the same place, e.g. two narrative beats both citing the
    // crossing verse) so neither a hover-verse-{VREF} testid nor a human
    // ever sees the same verse twice. This is the list design-direction.md
    // and this file have both long called "canonical order" -- Groups()
    // below groups THIS list's own order, not a fresh sort (see file
    // header).
    private List<string> MergedVerses()
    {
        var seen = new HashSet<string>();
        var result = new List<string>();

        foreach (var e in Place.Events)
        {
            foreach (var g in e.VerseGroups)
            {
                foreach (var v in g.Verses)
                {
                    if (seen.Add(v))
                    {
                        result.Add(v);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    // Splits `verses` into maximal runs of numerically-consecutive same-
    // book/chapter verses (Requirement 2: "same book+chapter, verse
    // numbers n, n+1, ..."). Adjacency is checked purely by LIST POSITION
    // -- see the file header's note on why that's the deliberate reading
    // of "the scene's activating verse list... canonical order", not a
    // fresh sort.
    private static List<VerseRun> Groups(List<string> verses) => PassageGrouping.Groups(verses);

    // Requirement 2's 4/2-initial rule: up to the first 4 verses of the
    // FIRST group if it's a passage; otherwise (the first group is a lone
    // verse -- by construction non-consecutive with whatever comes next)
    // the first 2 verses of the flat list, "2 non-consecutive verses" per
    // the brief. Only ever looks at the first group -- a short first
    // passage never pads out into the second group, a lone first verse
    // never reaches for a 3rd ("Only the first group (passage) or first
    // two lone verses appear initially -- never a wall").
    private static int InitialShownCount(List<string> verses)
    {
        if (verses.Count == 0)
        {
            return 0;
        }

        var first = Groups(verses)[0];
        return first.IsPassage ? Math.Min(PassageInitialMax, first.Length) : Math.Min(LoneInitialMax, verses.Count);
    }

    // Clips `groups` to however many verses are currently shown: every
    // group that starts before _shownCount is included, its own Length
    // clipped down to whatever portion of it is actually visible yet. A
    // pure function of (groups, shownCount), recomputed fresh every render
    // -- so a group that's only 1 verse into its reveal renders as a lone
    // verse now (visible Length 1, IsPassage false) and upgrades to a
    // passage block automatically once a later click reveals a 2nd verse
    // of it, with no separate reveal-history state to keep in sync.
    private static List<VerseRun> VisibleGroups(List<VerseRun> groups, int shownCount)
    {
        var visible = new List<VerseRun>();
        foreach (var g in groups)
        {
            if (g.Start >= shownCount)
            {
                break;
            }

            visible.Add(new VerseRun(g.Start, Math.Min(g.Length, shownCount - g.Start)));
        }

        return visible;
    }

    // The single ref label for a passage block (Requirement 2: "one ref
    // label for the block (span form, e.g. GEN.12.1-4)"). first/last are
    // always same book+chapter by construction (Groups only ever runs
    // within one book+chapter) -- reflects only whatever portion of the
    // underlying group is currently visible, so a partially-revealed 8-verse
    // passage shown 4-deep correctly spans "...1-4", not "...1-8".
    private static string SpanRef(string firstVref, string lastVref) => PassageGrouping.SpanRef(firstVref, lastVref);

    private static int VerseNumOf(string vref) => CanonRef.ParseVerse(vref).Verse;

    private string VerseText(string vref) => _verseText.TryGetValue(vref, out var text) ? text : "";

    // Fires on every parameter push, including this component's very first
    // render (Place.Id vacuously differs from the null _lastPlaceId then
    // too) -- the _lastPlaceId guard makes both cases, and an X/Y-only
    // update from the SAME place (no re-fetch needed), converge correctly.
    // Awaiting the fetch here (rather than firing it off unobserved) is
    // deliberate: Blazor renders once synchronously right after parameters
    // are applied and this method's synchronous prefix runs, THEN again
    // when the awaited task completes -- so the place name and every
    // hover-verse-{VREF}/hover-passage-{SPAN} ref (MergedVerses/Groups/
    // VisibleGroups are pure, synchronous) paint immediately, and
    // VerseText(vref) simply reads "" until the second render fills it in.
    // Same pattern ExplorerPopover's OnInitializedAsync/LoadCurrent already
    // relies on for its own body.
    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        if (Place.Id == _lastPlaceId)
        {
            return;
        }

        _lastPlaceId = Place.Id;
        _verseText.Clear();
        _history = null;
        // HOTFIX batch: a genuinely new place always starts back at the
        // default (unmeasured) placement -- see CardDxCss/CardDyCss's own
        // _placedForId comparison, which already treats "not yet measured
        // for THIS place" as the default regardless of whatever _dxPx/
        // _flipped happen to still hold from the PREVIOUS place, so those
        // two don't need resetting here too. _contentReady DOES need an
        // explicit reset: OnAfterRenderAsync's own guard checks it directly
        // (not via an id comparison), so leaving it true from a previous
        // place would let a measurement fire against THIS place's
        // synchronous-prefix-only render, before its own blurb/dates have
        // had any chance to load -- exactly the under-measurement this
        // batch exists to prevent.
        _contentReady = false;
        var verses = MergedVerses();
        _initialShownCount = InitialShownCount(verses);
        _shownCount = _initialShownCount;
        // Independent fetches (verse text vs. Batch E history vs. fix-round-1's
        // own narrative positions) run concurrently -- none depends on
        // another, and a slow/failed one must not delay or break the others
        // (each has its own try/catch, see LoadChaptersFor's/LoadHistory's/
        // LoadNarrativePositions' own comments).
        await Task.WhenAll(LoadChaptersFor(verses.Take(_shownCount)), LoadHistory(), LoadNarrativePositions());

        // HOTFIX batch: only NOW does this place's own rendered content
        // (verse text filled in, blurb/dates sections present-or-absent per
        // LoadHistory's own result) reflect what it will actually settle
        // at -- the earliest safe point for OnAfterRenderAsync's own
        // post-render measurement to trust what it sees. Setting this here
        // (rather than leaving it to a separate lifecycle hook) means
        // Blazor's own implicit re-render-after-this-method-completes is
        // what carries the news to OnAfterRenderAsync, the same "state
        // change in an async lifecycle method triggers its own render, no
        // manual StateHasChanged needed" shape as every other WhenAll-await
        // in this codebase.
        _contentReady = true;
    }

    // HOTFIX batch (requirement 1): fires after EVERY render, including the
    // two (at least) a genuinely new place always produces -- see
    // OnParametersSetAsync's own comment for exactly which one this must
    // wait for. Measures ONCE per place (the _placedForId guard below) and
    // never again for that same open, matching the brief's own "flip
    // decision at open time... recompute on re-open, not on pan": nothing
    // here re-fires just because ShowMore/Collapse/a later render changes
    // this card's own height afterward, by design.
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!_contentReady || _placedForId == Place.Id)
        {
            return;
        }

        // Captured before the await below -- if a NEWER place supersedes
        // this one while MeasureCardPlacement is still in flight (a fast
        // marker-to-marker hover, or GoToAdjacent's own re-pin),
        // OnParametersSetAsync above has already reset _contentReady for
        // that newer place by the time this continuation resumes; comparing
        // against the CURRENT Place.Id after the await (not this captured
        // copy) is what actually detects that and drops the now-stale
        // result -- same "compare a captured id against current state after
        // an await" shape World.razor's own GoToAdjacent/OpenPlaceFromCard
        // and MiniWorld.razor's own _disposed dance already use elsewhere
        // in this codebase for the identical class of race.
        var placeId = Place.Id;
        CardMeasurementDto measurement;
        try
        {
            measurement = await MapInterop.MeasureCardPlacement(JS, _cardEl);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Positioning is a nicety, not load-bearing -- same graceful-
            // degradation stance every other JS interop call on this card
            // takes (LoadChaptersFor/LoadHistory's own try/catch): a failed
            // measurement just leaves the card at its default above-anchor
            // placement, exactly what every card rendered before this
            // batch, never an unhandled exception taking down the card.
            return;
        }

        if (placeId != Place.Id)
        {
            // Stale -- a newer place already superseded this measurement
            // while it was in flight. Drop it; THAT place's own
            // OnAfterRenderAsync (once ITS content settles) measures fresh.
            return;
        }

        var (dx, dyAdjust, flipped) = CardPlacement.Compute(X, Y, measurement.Width, measurement.Height, measurement.ContainerWidth, measurement.ContainerHeight);
        _dxPx = dx;
        _dyAdjustPx = dyAdjust;
        _flipped = flipped;
        _placedForId = placeId;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    // Batch E: fetches this place's window-scoped history (blurb/dates) via
    // AtlasClient's own LRU cache. A nicety, not load-bearing -- same
    // graceful degradation as LoadChaptersFor: a failure just leaves
    // _history null, i.e. no blurb/dates section, never an unhandled
    // exception taking down the card.
    private async Task LoadHistory()
    {
        try
        {
            var detail = await Atlas.PlaceHistory(Place.Id, WindowFrom, WindowTo);
            _history = detail.History;
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
        }
    }

    // Fix round 1 (batch-n-review.md Critical-1): fetches this place's own
    // narrative position(s) via the SAME full-chain resolver
    // (atlas_core::narrative::positions_for_events, reached here through
    // AtlasClient.NarrativeEventPositions -- GET /api/narrative/event/{id})
    // the verse popover's own PRIOR/FOLLOWING sections already consume --
    // keyed by EVERY event id currently shown at this place (Place.Events),
    // since the endpoint is event-id-keyed, not place-keyed (one call per
    // event; typically 1-3 per place, the same order of magnitude as the
    // per-hover Chapter/PlaceHistory fetches this card already makes).
    //
    // This REPLACES the pre-fix-round-1 windowed-Arrows/PickAdjacent
    // adjacency derivation entirely (see NarrativeRows() below). The review
    // proved the old approach could disagree with the popover: a real,
    // ordinary time-slider window can end inside the exodus narrative's own
    // documented 37-year gap between ex_kadesh (-1445..-1444) and ex_moab
    // (-1407..-1406) (data/curated/events-extra.toml) -- scene.rs's
    // build_arrows filters each narrative's legs down to the window BEFORE
    // pairing adjacent ones (`kept.windows(2)`), so an out-of-window leg is
    // silently dropped from the card's own "next event" even though the
    // SAME window's verse popover (server-side positions_for_events,
    // deliberately unwindowed -- narrative.rs's own doc comment) still finds
    // it. Reading the identical full-chain resolver here means the two
    // surfaces can no longer disagree on order -- "one adjacency
    // computation, both surfaces consume it," per the owner's own "traverse
    // arbitrarily far... recursively" framing of the graph.
    //
    // Skips any synthetic "mention-{place}" pseudo-event id (scripture-mode
    // only -- see scene::compose_scripture_scene's own doc comment): never a
    // real event, so /api/narrative/event/{id} would 404 for it. Harmless
    // even without this filter (a place lit via a mention-* event is, by
    // that same function's own construction, never ALSO lit by a real one,
    // so Place.Events there is that single synthetic id alone, and the
    // catch below would just leave _narrativePositions empty -- correct
    // either way, since a mention-* pseudo-event is never a narrative leg)
    // -- filtered explicitly anyway so the common scripture-mode case never
    // relies on an exception for its own control flow.
    private async Task LoadNarrativePositions()
    {
        var eventIds = Place.Events.Select(e => e.Id).Where(id => !id.StartsWith("mention-", StringComparison.Ordinal)).ToList();
        if (eventIds.Count == 0)
        {
            _narrativePositions = new List<NarrativePositionDto>();
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            // Batch HOTFIX-4: each result now also carries a `.Timeline`
            // half (unused here -- TRAVERSAL-1's own `place-card-narratives`
            // row is narrative-scoped only, req 3's own map-side traversal
            // lives on the EVENT POPOVER, a separate surface; see
            // EventTimelinePriorSection/EventTimelineFollowingSection) --
            // `.Narrative` is the exact same list this method already read.
            var results = await Task.WhenAll(eventIds.Select(Atlas.NarrativeEventPositions));
            _narrativePositions = results.SelectMany(r => r.Narrative).ToList();
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // A nicety, not load-bearing -- same graceful degradation as
            // LoadHistory/LoadChaptersFor: a failed fetch just leaves no
            // narrative rows for this open, never an unhandled exception
            // taking down the card.
            _narrativePositions = new List<NarrativePositionDto>();
        }
    }

    // Shared with Explore.YearNode's own title text (via YearText.FormatClaim)
    // so the card's instrument-face line and the popover it opens always
    // agree on exactly the same date text.
    private static string DateText(DateClaimOut claim) => YearText.FormatClaim(claim.When.FromYear, claim.When.ToYear, claim.Note);

    private static string ExploreLabel(string label, DateClaimOut claim) => $"Explore {label.ToLowerInvariant()} date: {DateText(claim)}";

    // Two named wrappers (rather than one Explore(label, claim) called
    // inline with a string-literal argument) -- Razor's @onclick="..."
    // attribute-value parser cannot handle an embedded double-quoted C#
    // string literal inside the lambda (confirmed: it broke the generated
    // code, consuming the closing quote early). Named methods sidestep it
    // entirely; no inline string literals appear in the markup at all.
    private Task ExploreEstablished(DateClaimOut claim) =>
        OnExploreNode.InvokeAsync(new YearNode("Established", claim.When, claim.Verses, claim.Note));

    private Task ExploreDestroyed(DateClaimOut claim) =>
        OnExploreNode.InvokeAsync(new YearNode("Destroyed", claim.When, claim.Verses, claim.Note));

    // Batch G1 requirement 1b: card verses/passages become explorable via
    // the SAME OnExploreNode callback Batch E's dates already use (World
    // .razor's OpenExploreNode -- push the node, close this hover card the
    // way a promoted popover always supersedes it; see this callback's own
    // parameter comment).
    private Task ExploreVerse(string vref) => OnExploreNode.InvokeAsync(new VerseNode(vref));

    // The passage's own text, concatenated in verse order -- same "already-
    // in-memory, no second network round-trip" approach Reader.razor's own
    // OpenPassage/PassageNode already use (see PassageNode.cs's doc
    // comment); this card already fetched every visible verse's text via
    // LoadChaptersFor.
    private Task ExplorePassage(string span, List<string> blockVerses) =>
        OnExploreNode.InvokeAsync(new PassageNode(span, string.Join(" ", blockVerses.Select(VerseText))));

    // Keyboard equivalents (design-direction.md: "Enter opens" -- see
    // Reader.razor's OnVerseLineKeyDown for why Enter only, not Space, is
    // this batch's deliberate scope everywhere the interaction rule
    // applies).
    private Task OnVerseKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e, string vref) =>
        e.Key == "Enter" ? ExploreVerse(vref) : Task.CompletedTask;

    private Task OnPassageKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e, string span, List<string> blockVerses) =>
        e.Key == "Enter" ? ExplorePassage(span, blockVerses) : Task.CompletedTask;

    // place-card-more: reveals the next chunk. Step size is +5 if the next
    // not-yet-shown verse belongs to a passage-sized group (Length >= 2,
    // "...continuing inside a passage" -- true whether that group was
    // already partway open or this click starts it fresh, the two read the
    // same from here), +2 if it's a lone verse's group -- Requirement 2's
    // 5/2-step rule. A flat verse-count step, not capped at the group's own
    // remaining length, so one click can finish a passage and spill into
    // the next group's first verse or two in the same step; see
    // VisibleGroups' own comment for why that's safe to render.
    private async Task ShowMore()
    {
        var verses = MergedVerses();
        if (_shownCount >= verses.Count)
        {
            return;
        }

        var group = Groups(verses).First(g => _shownCount >= g.Start && _shownCount < g.Start + g.Length);
        var step = group.IsPassage ? PassageStep : LoneStep;
        var oldShown = _shownCount;
        _shownCount = Math.Min(_shownCount + step, verses.Count);
        await LoadChaptersFor(verses.Skip(oldShown).Take(_shownCount - oldShown));
    }

    // place-card-collapse: snaps back to the initial 4/2-count state in one
    // click (Requirement 2's up-arrow rule) -- no fetch needed, every verse
    // in the initial range was already loaded by OnParametersSetAsync (or
    // an earlier ShowMore).
    private void Collapse()
    {
        _shownCount = _initialShownCount;
    }

    // Batch G1 requirement 3: one row per narrative (colored swatch + name)
    // with its own prev/next adjacency.
    //
    // Fix round 1 (batch-n-review.md Critical-1): now reads _narrativePositions
    // (LoadNarrativePositions above) -- the SAME full-chain resolver the
    // verse popover's own PRIOR/FOLLOWING sections consume -- instead of the
    // current scene's WINDOWED Arrows list. Row EXISTENCE is therefore also
    // "this place has >=1 currently-shown event that's a narrative leg,"
    // rather than "this place has >=1 arrow endpoint in the current window"
    // -- a strict improvement: a genuinely isolated in-window leg whose
    // immediate chain-neighbors are BOTH out of window produced zero arrows
    // at all under the old scheme (build_arrows needs two CONSECUTIVE kept
    // legs to ever emit one), which silently hid the row entirely even
    // though the event itself was plainly on screen.
    //
    // Tie-break for a place revisited more than once by the SAME narrative
    // (a real, documented case -- see the pre-fix-round-1 version of this
    // comment/PickAdjacent for the old rule): FIRST-ENCOUNTERED wins, in
    // Place.Events' own display order. Simpler than the old "prefer the
    // shown event, else lowest arrow order" rule, and sufficient now that
    // EVERY candidate position already corresponds to a currently-shown
    // event by construction (each was fetched BY one of Place.Events' own
    // ids) -- the old rule's first clause no longer discriminates anything.
    private sealed record NarrativeAdjacency(string NarrativeId, string Name, string Color, string? PrevPlaceId, string? NextPlaceId);

    // Legend/SceneNarrative color is scoped to narratives with >=1 arrow
    // SOMEWHERE in the current scene (scene.rs's own legend()) -- true for
    // every narrative reachable through this method's own repro window, but
    // not guaranteed for a genuinely isolated single in-window leg whose
    // neighbors are both out of window (see the header comment above). This
    // neutral fallback keeps the row rendering -- name and traversal both
    // still work, neither depends on Narratives -- rather than losing real
    // PRIOR/FOLLOWING data over a missing swatch color.
    private const string DefaultNarrativeColor = "#8A8A8A";

    private List<NarrativeAdjacency> NarrativeRows()
    {
        var rows = new List<NarrativeAdjacency>();
        var seenNarratives = new HashSet<string>();

        foreach (var e in Place.Events)
        {
            foreach (var position in _narrativePositions.Where(p => p.EventId == e.Id))
            {
                if (!seenNarratives.Add(position.NarrativeId))
                {
                    continue; // first-encountered wins -- see header comment
                }

                var color = Narratives?.FirstOrDefault(n => n.Id == position.NarrativeId)?.Color ?? DefaultNarrativeColor;
                rows.Add(new NarrativeAdjacency(
                    position.NarrativeId,
                    position.NarrativeName,
                    color,
                    AdjacentPlaceId(position.Prior),
                    AdjacentPlaceId(position.Following)));
            }
        }

        return rows;
    }

    // NarrativeAdjacentEventDto.Places mirrors scene.rs's build_arrows' own
    // "a.places[0] is the anchor place" convention (see the Rust
    // NarrativeAdjacentEvent's own doc comment) -- the FIRST place is where
    // the card's own ‹ previous event / next event › control jumps to, same
    // as an arrow's own from_place/to_place always resolves today. Null (no
    // button, conditional presence) only if the adjacent event genuinely
    // carries no place at all -- unreachable in the real compiled data
    // (every curated event has >=1 place) but guarded rather than assumed,
    // the same "trust but verify" stance this codebase already takes for an
    // identical shape server-side (narrative.rs's own adjacent_event).
    private static string? AdjacentPlaceId(NarrativeAdjacentEventDto? adjacent) =>
        adjacent?.Places is { Count: > 0 } places ? places[0] : null;

    // Resolves every (book, chapter) pair the given verses span via
    // AtlasClient's own Chapter LRU cache -- a hover that revisits a place,
    // or a show-more that reopens a chapter an earlier step already
    // touched, never re-hits the network. Populates every verse FROM each
    // fetched chapter, not just the ones asked for: harmless (VerseText
    // only ever reads keys it knows to look up) and means several
    // requested verses sharing a chapter cost exactly one fetch.
    private async Task LoadChaptersFor(IEnumerable<string> vrefs)
    {
        var pairs = vrefs.Select(CanonRef.ParseVerse).Select(p => (p.Book, p.Chapter)).Distinct().ToList();
        if (pairs.Count == 0)
        {
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            var chapters = await Task.WhenAll(pairs.Select(p => Atlas.Chapter(p.Book, p.Chapter)));
            foreach (var chapter in chapters)
            {
                // chapter.Ref is the CODE-based "JDG.3" (atlas-server's
                // ChapterOut.sref, built from book.code()); chapter.Book is
                // the human-readable display NAME ("Judges", book.name()) --
                // a real, deliberate API distinction (VerseGroup.Book is the
                // code; ChapterOut.Book is the name), not interchangeable.
                // Building the key from Book here silently produced
                // "Judges.3.7" instead of "JDG.3.7", so it could never match
                // a hover-verse-{VREF} testid -- caught by
                // world-hover-text.spec.ts (verse text never appeared).
                foreach (var v in chapter.Verses)
                {
                    _verseText[$"{chapter.Ref}.{v.Verse}"] = v.Text;
                }
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // A hover card's verse text is a nicety, not load-bearing -- the
            // ref already rendered synchronously above; leaving its text
            // blank on a flaky-network failure degrades gracefully instead
            // of an unhandled exception from this lifecycle continuation
            // taking down the whole app.
        }
    }
}
